--- Spring-drags a body toward a world target (initially the grab point). Rotation is left free, matching classic mouse-joint feel. Default max_force scales with the body's mass so heavy and light bodies feel the same.
---@param body lightuserdata|table
---@param px any
---@param py any
---@param pz any
---@param hertz__damping__max_force? any
function physics3_create_grab_joint(body, px, py, pz, hertz__damping__max_force) end

--- joint Ball-and-socket at world point (ax, ay, az), with optional cone and twist limits and a rotational spring. The spring is the point: set `hertz` and then feed physics3_joint_set_target_rotation() the rotation the ANIMATION wants between the two bodies, and the joint drives the limb back to the pose after something knocks it away. That's a powered ragdoll with no state machine — the recovery is the spring, not a scripted blend. Both local frames are built with identity rotation, so "frame B relative to frame A" is just inverse(rotA)*rotB. A target of identity therefore means "both bodies aligned as they were when the joint was made" — i.e. the rest pose, if you build the joints in it. opts: cone (radians, <=0 disables), twist_lo, twist_hi (radians, equal disables), hertz, damping, collide (default false)
---@param bodyA any
---@param bodyB any
---@param ax number
---@param ay number
---@param az number
---@param opts? table
---@return any
function physics3_create_spherical_joint(bodyA, bodyB, ax, ay, az, opts) end

--- The rotation the spring pulls frame B toward, relative to frame A. Fed the animated pose every frame, this is what makes a ragdoll return to its animation instead of hanging limp.
---@param joint lightuserdata|table
---@param qx any
---@param qy any
---@param qz any
---@param qw any
function physics3_joint_set_target_rotation(joint, qx, qy, qz, qw) end

--- live stiffness tuning. Hertz spans the whole range from "rigid animated character that barely flinches" to "floppy ragdoll", which is why it's worth having on a key.
---@param joint lightuserdata|table
---@param hertz any
---@param damping number
function physics3_joint_set_spring(joint, hertz, damping) end

