--- mesh `vertices` is a flat array of floats, 8 per vertex: x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, u,v. Non-indexed triangles, so the length must be a multiple of 24. Under headless this returns 0 — a valid-looking handle that is never `alive`, so every downstream draw no-ops instead of forcing nil guards in game code.
---@param vertices number[]
---@return any
function mesh3_create(vertices) end

--- mesh All three are BINARY STRINGS, not tables: a skinned character is tens of thousands of floats and pushing them one Lua value at a time costs more than the skinning itself. Layout, per vertex: vertex_blob  8 x float32   x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, u,v   (the rest pose) index_blob   4 x uint8     bone indices weight_blob  4 x float32   weights, expected to sum to 1 Headless returns 0, matching mesh3_create: a handle that is never alive, so downstream draws no-op instead of forcing nil guards in game code.
---@param vertex_blob any
---@param index_blob any
---@param weight_blob any
---@return any
function mesh3_create_skinned(vertex_blob, index_blob, weight_blob) end

--- `matrices` is a flat table of 16*n numbers, column-major mat4 per bone, already premultiplied by the inverse rest pose. Blends the rest vertices and re-uploads. Call once per character per frame, before the layer3_mesh that draws it.
---@param mesh lightuserdata|table
---@param matrices any
function mesh3_skin(mesh, matrices) end

--- free a custom mesh id from mesh3_create_*
---@param mesh integer
function mesh3_destroy(mesh) end

--- texture is a Texture handle from texture_load (nil clears it back to the shared white texture).
